<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhrikuti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Bhrikuti Devi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhrikuti is the Nepalese princess who, according to Tibetan tradition, married the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> in the seventh century, alongside the Tang princess Wencheng. Tradition credits her with bringing Buddhist images to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and with a part in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, and she is revered as an emanation of Green Tara. Her historicity is debated, as she is absent from the earliest sources.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhrikuti.jpg" alt="Bhrikuti" width="307" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Stavro Blofeld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhrikuti_Devi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> know Bhrikuti Devi as Bal-mo-bza' Khri-btsun, the Nepali consort, or simply Khri-btsun, the royal lady. Tradition makes her a princess of the Licchavi dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and the first queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from about 622. No reference to her has been found among the documents discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, but Tibet and Nepal were certainly in close contact at the time, and some scholars hold that such a marriage is historically likely.</p><p>Many Tibetan accounts call her a daughter of Amshuvarma (605–621), co-ruler and successor of Śivadeva I; if so, the marriage must have taken place before 624, although Acharya Kirti Tulku Lobsang Tenzin dates it to 632. Other legends name her father as a king called Go Cha, whom Sylvain Lévi identified as Udayavarman, most likely the Udayadeva who was Amshuvarma's adopted heir. That would make her a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendradeva">Narendradeva</a>, who took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and, according to the Old Book of Tang, was restored to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> with Tibetan help in 641.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tibetan tradition holds that Bhrikuti, a devout Buddhist, brought sacred images and skilled Newar craftsmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> as part of her dowry. The Testament of Ba links her marriage to the building of the temple of Rasa, and she and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> are credited with the Tsulag Khang, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a>, built to house the images. They are also credited with the Red Palace on Marpo Ri, built by Nepali craftsmen, which moved the seat of government from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung Valley</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and was later rebuilt as the Potala by the Fifth Dalai Lama.</p><p>Her statue, the Jowo Mikyo Dorje, also called the Manuvajra, came from Bodhgaya and, according to the 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, rested at Swayambhunath for seven months before reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. It is now in the Ramoche Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. It is said that after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> was gutted in the 1960s the statue's lower half was found in a Lhasa rubbish dump and its upper half in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and the parts were joined again. In Tibetan iconography Bhrikuti is usually shown as Green Tara, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-wencheng">Princess Wencheng</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> married in 641, as White Tara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhrikuti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tibetan tradition adds a Nepalese wife, <b>Bhrikuti</b>, whose historicity is debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
